 weeks the  entire jobs recovery from the recession  including the jobs created under Barack  Obama and the roughly six million that  were created under Donald Trump up until  this started all of those jobs are gone  so that's number one the magnitude of it  number two every one of us that has a  job should be very grateful because the  situation is truly horrifying that that  that's just an interjection even people  who have lost jobs and want to go on  unemployment insurance or having trouble  so it's not as simple as well you lose  your job the government's going to bail  you out there are states that may soon  or not run out of unemployment money  many people just aren't hearing back for  weeks  many people filed or attempted to file  weeks ago they haven't gotten a dollar  yet because the systems are so  overwhelmed truly dystopian now in terms  of the outlook even if new claims slow  down and they probably will right so we  may go down to only 3.8 million new  claims next week and maybe 3.4 the fault  right you could have a declining growth  rate the total number of people on  unemployment could continue climbing in  total over the next few months depending  on the spread of the pandemic we could  have 30 million we could have 35 million  I spoke to one economist who thinks by  July depending on how things go we could  have 40 million people on unemployment  benefits can the states even afford that  it's not clear now you have a contingent  from the right that says just open it up  and it'll all get resolved because the  economy will restart doesn't seem to be  the case because unless and until you  deal with the pandemic when you you can  open a restaurant you can open a barber  shop you can open a nail salon doesn't  mean that people are going to go it's  not going to fix this if here in  Massachusetts right now salons and  barbershops opened up I'm not going to  get a haircut nobody have spoken to is  going to get a haircut  what good is taking someone off 